Subject: [PATCH 6/7] relax: a by-value use of a temporary Box's contents drops
 them once

`Box::from_raw(p).wake()` and `make_box().consume()` take `self` by value
through the autoderef of a temporary Box. The receiver was lowered as a place
read: the contents were copied out for the callee, and the temporary Box kept
its full drop at the end of the statement, so the boxed value was dropped a
second time after the callee had consumed it.

futures-util's BiLock::unlock is `Box::from_raw(n).wake()`. With the two halves
of a split WebSocket contending, the parked half's Waker was woken by value and
then dropped again, which took one reference too many off its tokio task:
octos panicked at start with "current: 1, sub: 2" (tokio task/state.rs:200) in
about 3 of 4 starts under a client that connects at once, and aborted in
ref_inc after a long session.

The receiver is now taken as `*tmp` already is in lower_expr: the temporary is
evaluated by value, the contents are read, and each allocation is freed without
them (box_free_after_move), outermost first for Box<Box<T>>.

A field moved out through the same autoderef (`make_box().a`) leaked the rest
of the contents and the allocation. The contents now become the statement's
temporary with the moved field marked, as `(*make_box()).a` does.

tests/box_temp_move_out.rs: 4 of 4 on rustc and here, 1 of 4 before. The other
474 single-file tests give the same results on both builds. octos: 0 panics in
20 starts with wakers instrumented to catch a second give-back.

